1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,288
Sunday, a quarter past nine in the morning.

2
00:00:03,338 --> 00:00:08,880
At a bus stop, a buyer orders tulips from Malika's shop, and presses pay.

3
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:11,856
Malika's site does not ask for the card.

4
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:14,016
It sends the buyer to a link.

5
00:00:14,066 --> 00:00:24,168
In Lesson 25, ecom answered Bekzod's create request with that link, and in Lesson 5, the bank's page held the card frame from the card zone.

6
00:00:24,218 --> 00:00:29,064
This lesson is that page, built so that only this buyer can open it.

7
00:00:29,114 --> 00:00:34,704
Malika must never touch a card number, so the card cannot be typed on her site.

8
00:00:34,754 --> 00:00:40,608
And the page cannot sit at an address anyone could guess by changing a number.

9
00:00:40,658 --> 00:00:43,872
It must open only for the person who got the link.

10
00:00:43,922 --> 00:00:45,936
It must work only for a while.

11
00:00:45,986 --> 00:00:48,576
It must show exactly what is being paid.

12
00:00:48,626 --> 00:00:53,784
And it must survive a buyer with cold fingers who presses Confirm twice.

13
00:00:53,834 --> 00:01:00,056
How can a payment page open only for the buyer who got the link?

14
00:01:00,106 --> 00:01:05,864
When Bekzod's server creates the payment, the bank's answer carries the link.

15
00:01:05,914 --> 00:01:11,408
The link holds a page token: a long, random, one-time key.

16
00:01:11,458 --> 00:01:14,312
The bank does not keep the token itself.

17
00:01:14,362 --> 00:01:21,416
It keeps only a fingerprint of it, so even someone reading the bank's records cannot rebuild the link.

18
00:01:21,466 --> 00:01:24,944
And the link expires after the bank's chosen time.

19
00:01:24,994 --> 00:01:28,112
The buyer taps it, and the page loads.

20
00:01:28,162 --> 00:01:36,032
It shows Malika's website name and logo, the order, the amount, and the cards this website accepts.

21
00:01:36,082 --> 00:01:39,008
Those are the cards with a live terminal behind them.

22
00:01:39,058 --> 00:01:46,544
Then the page opens a card entry session with the card zone, and puts the card zone's frame inside the page.

23
00:01:46,594 --> 00:01:50,912
The card number goes into that frame, and only into that frame.

24
00:01:50,962 --> 00:01:55,184
A payment gets only the bank's chosen number of these sessions.

25
00:01:55,234 --> 00:01:59,456
After that, the payment fails, and no money moves.

26
00:01:59,506 --> 00:02:05,432
When the buyer finishes typing, the frame sends the page one small message.

27
00:02:05,482 --> 00:02:11,336
The page accepts it only from the frame's own address, and only if it holds a token.

28
00:02:11,386 --> 00:02:13,568
It never accepts a card number.

29
00:02:13,618 --> 00:02:16,160
The page passes the token to ecom.

30
00:02:16,210 --> 00:02:20,792
ecom asks the card zone whether this card belongs to this payment's session.

31
00:02:20,842 --> 00:02:23,000
Only then does the charge begin.

32
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:28,472
And a page lock lets only one step run at a time for each payment.

33
00:02:28,522 --> 00:02:31,208
Here is the page on the buyer's phone.

34
00:02:31,258 --> 00:02:35,456
At the top, a line says secure payment page of the bank.

35
00:02:35,506 --> 00:02:44,432
Below it are the website, the order number, the amount to pay, the accepted cards, and a line that says valid until, with the time.

36
00:02:44,482 --> 00:02:47,024
Under that sits the card details frame.

37
00:02:47,074 --> 00:02:49,160
The buyer double clicks Confirm.

38
00:02:49,210 --> 00:02:50,960
The first click goes through.

39
00:02:51,010 --> 00:02:55,232
The second is turned away, because the page is busy.

40
00:02:55,282 --> 00:02:57,128
Only one step runs.

41
00:02:57,178 --> 00:03:04,208
Now suppose the bus arrives, and the frame stays open too long, or the buyer makes too many tries.

42
00:03:04,258 --> 00:03:08,480
The page says that attempt ended, and asks to enter the card again.

43
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:16,160
If every attempt is used up, the page says it closed before any money moved, and asks for a new link from the shop.

44
00:03:16,210 --> 00:03:21,032
A buyer who opens the link again next week sees that it has expired.

45
00:03:21,082 --> 00:03:25,448
And when the payment ends, the page shows a link back to the shop.

46
00:03:25,498 --> 00:03:28,232
Every one of these rules closes a door.

47
00:03:28,282 --> 00:03:34,184
Without the page token, anyone could walk through payment pages by guessing addresses.

48
00:03:34,234 --> 00:03:39,344
Without the fingerprint, a leaked copy of the bank's records would hand out live links.

49
00:03:39,394 --> 00:03:44,360
Without the frame, the card number would pass through ecom and Malika's shop.

50
00:03:44,410 --> 00:03:48,584
Without the lock, one double click could start two charges.

51
00:03:48,634 --> 00:03:50,456
My advice is simple.

52
00:03:50,506 --> 00:03:56,528
If your page address holds a number you can count up, you have built a page anyone can open.

53
00:03:56,578 --> 00:04:02,480
Here, the buyer sees exactly what they pay, and Malika never touches a card.

54
00:04:02,530 --> 00:04:09,728
To recap: a one-time token in the link, the card inside the bank's own frame, and one step at a time.

55
00:04:09,778 --> 00:04:13,432
So where does the card number go?

56
00:04:13,482 --> 00:04:15,544
Only into the card zone's frame.

57
00:04:15,594 --> 00:04:18,016
Next: the charge itself.
